= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a nonnegative <submartingale> $(M_k)_{0\leq k\leq n}$ and $M_n^*=\max_{0\leq k\leq n}M_k$, the weak form of the <Doob maximal inequality> is
$$
\boxed{u\,\mathbb P(M_n^*\geq u)\leq\mathbb E[M_n\mathbf1_{\{M_n^*\geq u\}}]\leq\mathbb E M_n\qquad(u>0).}
$$
To prove it, let $A_k$ be the event that $k$ is the first index with $M_k\geq u$. These events are disjoint, $A_k\in\mathcal F_k$, and their union is $A=\{M_n^*\geq u\}$. The <submartingale> property gives $\mathbb E[M_n\mid\mathcal F_k]\geq M_k$. Multiplying by $\mathbf1_{A_k}$, taking expectations and summing yields $\mathbb E[M_n\mathbf1_A]\geq\sum_k\mathbb E[M_k\mathbf1_{A_k}]\geq u\mathbb P(A)$. Nonnegativity gives the second inequality.

The associated <Doob Lp maximal inequality>, for $p>1$ and $M_n\in L^p$, is
$$
\boxed{\|M_n^*\|_p\leq\frac p{p-1}\|M_n\|_p.}
$$
Indeed $M_k\leq\mathbb E[M_n\mid\mathcal F_k]$, so conditional <Jensen inequality> first ensures finite $p$th moments for all $M_k$ and hence for the finite maximum. Integrating the weak inequality against $pu^{p-2}\,du$ and using the <Tonelli theorem> gives
$$
\mathbb E(M_n^*)^p\leq\frac p{p-1}\mathbb E[M_n(M_n^*)^{p-1}]
\leq\frac p{p-1}\|M_n\|_p\|M_n^*\|_p^{p-1}.
$$
The last step is <Hölder inequality>. Dividing, unless the maximum is zero, proves the stated norm bound. For an arbitrary <martingale>, its absolute value is a nonnegative <submartingale> by conditional <Jensen inequality>, so both corresponding absolute-maximum bounds follow.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <moment-generating function of a normal distribution> gives
$$
\mathbb E e^{\lambda X_1}=\exp\left(-\lambda+\frac{\lambda^2}{2}\right).
$$
Independence of $X_{n+1}$ from $\mathcal F_n$ implies
$$
\mathbb E[e^{\lambda S_{n+1}}\mid\mathcal F_n]=e^{\lambda S_n}\exp\left(-\lambda+\frac{\lambda^2}{2}\right).
$$
Every exponential here is integrable. Thus the process is a <martingale> exactly when $-\lambda+\lambda^2/2=0$. Its two roots are $0$ and $2$, and the unique positive root is
$$
\boxed{\lambda=2.}
$$
The resulting <exponential martingale of a random walk> is nonnegative and has <expectation> $1$ at every finite time.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <strong law of large numbers> gives $S_n/n\to-1$ almost surely, so $S_n\to-\infty$. Therefore the infinite-horizon maximum $Z$ is finite and attained almost surely; also $Z\geq0$ because $S_0=0$.

Apply the <Doob maximal inequality> to $M_k=e^{2S_k}$ through time $n$. Since $\mathbb E M_n=1$,
$$
\mathbb P\left(\max_{0\leq k\leq n}S_k>t\right)\leq e^{-2t}\qquad(t\geq0).
$$
These finite-horizon events increase to $\{Z>t\}$. Continuity of <probability> under increasing unions gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(Z>t)\leq e^{-2t}=e^{-\lambda t}.}
$$
For $t<0$ the same bound is trivial, because its right side exceeds $1$. Only bounded-horizon maximal inequalities were used before passing to the limit; no unjustified stopping at an infinite-horizon crossing time is needed.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $0<b<\lambda=2$, integrate the identity $e^{bz}-1=\int_0^z be^{bt}\,dt$ for $z\geq0$. The <Tonelli theorem> and the tail bound in (c) give
$$
\mathbb E e^{bZ}=1+b\int_0^\infty e^{bt}\mathbb P(Z>t)\,dt
\leq1+b\int_0^\infty e^{-(2-b)t}\,dt=\frac2{2-b}.
$$
For $b=0$ the <expectation> is $1$, and for $b<0$ the fact $Z\geq0$ gives $0<e^{bZ}\leq1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E e^{bZ}<\infty\quad\text{for every }b<2.}
$$
This is an <exponential moment bound for a Gaussian random-walk maximum>. The upper-tail bound proves the requested sufficient range; it alone does not establish a converse at the endpoint.

